A complex number is represented as a CT_ARRAY of two reals, so the width of the
value decides which pair of conversions converts it back. Only the three widths
libffi provides complex types for are representable; any other width must not
fall through to the narrowest one, whose conversions are themselves complex and
would re-enter the representation with the same width until the stack is
exhausted, so it is reported instead.

Represent the complex types of the widths libffi provides.

-- Testcase --
{%
    import * as ffi from 'ffi';

    // Only the three widths libffi provides complex types for can be converted
    // back. Any other width must not fall through to the narrowest one, whose
    // conversions are themselves complex and would re-enter the
    // representation with the same width until the stack is exhausted.
    print("float:  ", ffi.ctype('float complex', 1.0, 2.0).tostring(), "\n");
    print("double: ", ffi.ctype('double complex', 1.0, 2.0).tostring(), "\n");
    print("long:   ", ffi.ctype('long double complex', 1.0, 2.0).tostring(), "\n");
%}
-- End --

-- Expect stdout --
float:  complex float: 1+2i
double: complex: 1+2i
long:   complex: 1+2i
-- End --
